# XSH setsockopt()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Options are set on fresh sockets (socketpair(), loopback AF_INET and,
# under IP6, AF_INET6) and read back with getsockopt(). Boolean options are
# set to a non-zero value other than 1 and to 0; only zero and non-zero are
# compared on the way back. An option that the protocol does not support
# (ENOPROTOOPT) leaves the test not in use, as the page lets a protocol
# leave options out; the page also leaves open whether SO_RCVTIMEO,
# SO_SNDTIMEO, SO_RCVLOWAT and SO_SNDLOWAT can be set at all. The values of
# SO_RCVBUF and SO_SNDBUF after a set are not specified, so only success is
# judged. Waiting-time tests have a lower bound only (the clock's
# resolution allowed for) and a guard alarm in place of an upper bound.
# Not tested: SO_DEBUG (may need privilege), SO_DONTROUTE effect, the
# effect of SO_LINGER, SO_REUSEADDR, SO_KEEPALIVE and SO_BROADCAST (all
# protocol-specific), SO_RCVLOWAT and SO_SNDLOWAT values, EISCONN (no
# option is known that cannot be set on a connected socket), ENOMEM and
# ENOBUFS (may-fail).

assertion  XSH.setsockopt.001
anchor     functions/setsockopt.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.10.16
class      required
since      2001
says       SO_REUSEADDR set to a non-zero value on a new TCP socket reads
           back non-zero, and set to 0 reads back 0.
test       t/001-reuseaddr.c

